BEGIN_DESC
Io, early dawn (the Sun ~3 deg up in the east): the heavy suit "Pantsir" (class 2) on the frost plain ~5 m from the front of an active pahoehoe lobe, southern margin of the Prometheus flow field (1.785 S, 153.616 W).
Ahead (NE): the lobe's breakout toes (minutes old, ~800-1100 C, glowing), behind them crust hours old: 1 m in ~780 C (4 mm crust), 5 m in ~490 C (4 cm), 15 m in ~380 C (9 cm), 100 m in ~260 C (a day old, 24 cm). Glowing cooling cracks on the crust, a lava tube with glowing skylights runs up NE to a small lava shield (~95 m) with a crusted lava lake in its summit pit.
Temperatures: 1-D cooling model of 1450 K basalt in vacuum (Tantra_Design/io_lava, Config/Io/LavaField). Tiles: Textures/Io Surf/Mask/Elev (procedural relief on the real site; elevation every 2.7 m).
Pantsir nominal is +430 C, the floating limit ~550-600 C: walk in and watch THERMAL CONTROL.
Live effects: the IoLava\LavaField object (no mesh) throws the lava's light on the suit and visor with a slow flicker (crust tearing, toe breakouts) and shows SO2 frost sublimating where the front overruns the plain. No heat shimmer: Io's ~1 nbar gas cannot bend light.
NOTE: the suit code does not read these lava temperatures yet (thermal.groundT still comes from the body's day/night table), so the soles will not heat or glow from the lava until the crew module loads Config/Io/LavaField (see Tantra_Design/io_lava/README.md).
The Prometheus plume (vent ~70 km W, drawn by the IoLava\LavaField object): a ballistic SO2 gas-and-frost umbrella 80 km high and 320 km across, optically thin - a faint bluish canopy overhead, brightest looking towards the Sun; at dawn it is sunlit before the ground. No smoke billows anywhere: in vacuum every grain flies a parabola. Jupiter is below the horizon on this side of Io.
8 m to her left (W, on the frost plain, ~10 m from the lava: the ground there radiates under 250 K, the EMPU's heat alarm is at 400 K): the EMPU, headlights off, and beyond it Kaira Rean in the Suit_T10 (set Boron) with the Hexa flight module, both facing the volcano.
END_DESC

